const FAQS = [
  {
    q: "Why do cats purr?",
    a: [
      "Cats purr for many reasons — contentment is the most familiar one, but they also purr to self-soothe when stressed, injured, or giving birth.",
      "The vibrations (around 25–150 Hz) are even thought to promote tissue healing and bone density."
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "How much sleep do cats actually need?",
    a: [
      "Adult cats typically sleep 12–16 hours a day, and kittens or seniors can clock 18–20 hours. Most of it is light dozing rather than deep sleep, so they can spring awake instantly."
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "Why does my cat knead with their paws?",
    a: [
      "Kneading is a leftover behavior from kittenhood, when paw-pressing stimulated milk flow from their mother.",
      "As adults, cats knead when they feel safe, comfortable, or affectionate — it's a strong sign of trust."
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "Are cats really nocturnal?",
    a: [
      "Cats are crepuscular, not nocturnal. They are most active at dawn and dusk, which matches the activity patterns of their natural prey."
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "Why do cats bring me 'gifts'?",
    a: [
      "It's instinct. In the wild, cats bring prey back to a safe place — and sometimes share it with kin. Your cat may see you as family or simply as a poor hunter who needs help."
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "How can I tell if my cat is happy?",
    a: [
      "Look for slow blinks, a softly upright tail with a slight curl, relaxed whiskers, kneading, and gentle purring.",
      "A happy cat seeks you out for short check-ins, eats well, plays, and uses the litter box reliably."
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "Is it safe to give cats milk?",
    a: [
      "Most adult cats are lactose intolerant, so cow's milk often causes digestive upset. Fresh water is what they really need; specially formulated cat milk is fine as an occasional treat."
    ]
  }
];

